WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.200
Hi, I'm Hap.

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:09.480
Today we're opening a terminal
inside Visual Studio Code,

3
00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:12.520
and learning how to check which
directory it's working in.

4
00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:17.440
You've got a project open
in Visual Studio Code,

5
00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:19.840
and something is telling
you to run a command.

6
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:24.480
That means you need a terminal, and
Visual Studio Code has one built in.

7
00:00:26.095 --> 00:00:29.135
Look along the menu bar for
the word Terminal, click it,

8
00:00:29.135 --> 00:00:32.015
and choose New Terminal from
the list that drops down.

9
00:00:32.015 --> 00:00:33.375
That's the route I'd start with,

10
00:00:33.375 --> 00:00:36.175
because it works the same
way on a Mac and on Windows.

11
00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:42.270
A panel opens across the bottom of the
window with a prompt waiting in it.

12
00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:45.630
That prompt is the terminal,
and it will sit there doing

13
00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:49.150
nothing at all until you type
something and press Enter.

14
00:00:49.939 --> 00:00:53.859
On Windows the first line will
probably mention PowerShell,

15
00:00:53.859 --> 00:00:57.059
which is normal, and your assignment
will tell you when to change it.

16
00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:01.750
Every terminal has a
current working directory.

17
00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:07.030
Directory is the word developers use
for what VS Code calls a folder,

18
00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:09.510
so when you read one you
can think the other.

19
00:01:09.995 --> 00:01:15.675
VS Code sets that directory for you, and
it uses whichever folder you have open.

20
00:01:15.995 --> 00:01:19.755
Look at the top of the sidebar,
at the name in capital letters.

21
00:01:19.915 --> 00:01:22.395
That is your terminal's
current working directory.

22
00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:27.310
The terminal has its own way
of telling you the same thing.

23
00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:28.750
The command is

24
00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:31.470
pwd, which is short for
print working directory,

25
00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:33.390
and you'll be typing it soon enough.

26
00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:36.350
The sidebar and that
command will always agree,

27
00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:38.510
because they're describing
the same directory.

28
00:01:40.165 --> 00:01:46.805
If you haven't opened a folder
at all, VS Code has no project
directory to hand the terminal,

29
00:01:46.805 --> 00:01:48.805
so it falls back to your home directory.

30
00:01:49.153 --> 00:01:54.193
Anything you create from there ends up
outside the project you meant to work in,

31
00:01:54.193 --> 00:01:57.633
which is not only a confusing
thing to find out later

32
00:01:57.633 --> 00:02:00.673
but can also create problems
with other files there.

33
00:02:02.421 --> 00:02:07.461
When that name isn't the project you had
in mind, go to File, then Open Folder,

34
00:02:07.461 --> 00:02:11.461
and choose the project folder itself
rather than the folder holding it.

35
00:02:11.701 --> 00:02:13.701
Open a new terminal afterwards

36
00:02:13.701 --> 00:02:16.341
and its working directory
will be the one you wanted.

37
00:02:16.936 --> 00:02:17.736
That's it.

38
00:02:17.736 --> 00:02:21.976
You've got a terminal, and you know how
to check its current working directory.

39
00:02:22.136 --> 00:02:24.056
Now you can run that first command.
